  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nA cold front moving through the region early this morning is \nbringing brisk northwesterly winds (gusts in the 20s to 50s mph) \nto many locations. Ahead of this front, windy south-easterly \nwinds exist over far eastern Montana early this morning due to a \nlow level jet. With this, wind gusts of 30 to 40 mph are common, \nbut isolated gusts into the 50s mph are possible as showers, and \nmaybe a few weak thunderstorms (15 percent chance), potentially \nduct stronger winds aloft down towards the surface at times \n(slight chance). Once the cold front makes its way through eastern\nMontana into the Dakotas by 4 to 7 AM MDT today, brisk \nnorthwesterly winds will exist across the region. Along with the \nwind, scattered precipitation will gradually move east through the\narea early this morning. As it does, the precipitation is \nexpected to gradually dissipate as the strong frontogenetic \nforcing is lost. Before it does, precipitation will occasionally \nswitch over to snow, mainly over the foothills west of Billings. \nLittle to no accumulation is expected with this activity though \ndue to warmer ground temperatures. \n\nToday will be a breezy and mostly dry day to start with only \nisolated showers around. Temperatures will be cooler with highs in\nthe 40s and 50s. By late this afternoon into the evening, a \nsecondary cold front looks to turn winds more out of the north. \nWhile subtle, it should be enough to allow more upslope flow into \nthe southern mountains and foothills, especially around the \nBeartooth and Red Lodge foothills. In these locations, sounding \nindicate the atmospheric profile will saturate near the surface, \nallowing additional precipitation to form. With this, a period of \nlight to moderate snow is forecast this evening into Monday over \nthe Beartooth and Red Lodge foothills. While amounts still vary \nbetween models, generally a trace to 4 inches are forecast with \nthe greatest amounts along the immediate foothills. Because of \nthis, the Winter Weather Advisory for the Beartooth and Red Lodge \nfoothills remains in effect from 6PM MDT today to noon MDT Monday.\nThe mountains also look to get into the action during this time \nwith 5 to 10 inches of snow forecast in the Absaroka-Beartooth \nMountains, 4 to 8 inches of snow forecast in the Bighorn \nMountains, and 2 to 5 inches in the Pryor Mountains. Because of \nthis, the Winter Weather Advisory for the Pryor and Bighorn \nMountains remains in effect from 6 PM MDT today to noon MDT Monday\nfor impacts to travel along Highway 14. On the east side of the \nBighorn Mountains, around Sheridan, WY, winds look to remain a \nlittle too downslope today into tonight, limiting precipitation \nfor the time being. \n\nMonday will be the coldest day of the week with high temperatures\ngenerally in the 40s. With these cooler temperatures, another \nround of scattered precipitation is in the forecast, especially \nfor north-central/eastern Wyoming and south-eastern Montana, as an\narea of low pressure wraps up over Wyoming and moves east into \nthe high plains. With snow levels hovering around 4000 feet, \nprecipitation looks to be a rain and snow mix over the plains. \nWhile dynamic cooling could favor snow over rain at times, \nespecially if the system tracks a bit closer to our area, surface \ntemperatures along with the May sun angle will limit any snow \naccumulations during the daytime hours. At this time, potential \nliquid precipitation amounts (rain or melted snow) are around a \ncouple tenths to a few hundredths of an inch for the locations \nmentioned above. The precipitation then comes to an end late \nMonday into Tuesday as shortwave ridging moves into the area. \n\nThe early week weather system will also bring frost and freeze \nconditions to many locations tonight into Monday morning and \nMonday night into Tuesday morning (coldest Monday night into \nTuesday morning).\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft with several embedded systems passing \nthrough the flow are then forecast for the second half of the week\ninto the weekend. With this, daytime high temperatures will warm \nback into the 70s with occasional chances of precipitation, mainly\nWednesday and Friday. Arends",
